      "question": "Is this a time-sensitive emergency, and why?",
      "my_answer": "Yes, this is a time-sensitive emergency.",
      "rationale": "Elevated lactate (3.1 mmol/L), leukocytosis, tachycardia, dehydration, and imaging suggesting a possible closed-loop obstruction indicate potential bowel ischemia, which can rapidly progress to strangulation and perforation without prompt intervention."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q3",
      "question": "Which clinical specialty should urgently evaluate this patient?",
      "my_answer": "General Surgery.",
      "rationale": "The diagnosis likely involves mechanical small bowel obstruction with possible closed-loop and ischemic features—conditions requiring urgent surgical evaluation and potential intervention."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q4",
      "question": "What are the key components of the differential diagnosis?",
      "my_answer": "Mechanical small bowel obstruction (most likely), partial vs. complete obstruction, ileus, colonic obstruction, intra-abdominal infection (e.g. appendicitis, diverticulitis), and ischemic bowel.",
      "rationale": "While SBO is the leading diagnosis due to surgical history and imaging, ileus may present similarly but typically lacks transition point and peritoneal signs. Ischemic bowel overlaps with lactate elevation, but transition point and fecal loading favor mechanical obstruction. Prior surgeries increase adhesion risk, supporting SBO."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q5",
      "question": "What red flags are present that increase concern for complications?",
      "my_answer": "Elevated lactate, leukocytosis, tachycardia, hypotension, inability to pass flatus or stool for 36 hours, bilious/feculent vomitus, involuntary guarding, and CT findings suggestive of closed-loop configuration.",
      "rationale": "These signs suggest systemic inflammation and possible bowel ischemia or impending perforation, all of which necessitate urgent surgical management."
